Q: Should I use std::rethrow_exception rather than directly rethrowing it? A: <p>I would assume that the IDE hint has been misinterpreted. I do not think that it wanted you to use a <code>std::rethrow_exception</code> construct, but to use a rethrow expression:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>try { do_stuff(); }
catch(my_exception_class&amp; e) {
    if (not can_ignore_exception(e)) {
        throw; // change made here
    }
    log_or_do_nothing(e);
}

</code></pre>
<p>And this form really has advantages, e.g. it maintains the original type of the exception.</p>

Q: How do I include another level of Python nested if statements to classify the wind as &quot;Calm&quot;, &quot;Breezy&quot; or &quot;Windy&quot;? A: <p>Correcting your code:</p>
<pre><code>temperature = float(input(&quot;Please enter the temperature in Celsius: &quot;))

  # put &quot;lower()&quot; in correct place
raining = input( &quot;Is it raining? Yes/No&quot; ).lower()

  # we need a number
wind_speed = int( input(&quot;Please enter the wind speed in km/h: &quot; ))

  # declare missing atributte
wind_type = &quot;&quot;

if temperature &gt;= 25:
    condition = &quot;Hot&quot;
    if raining == &quot;yes&quot;:
        condition += &quot; and raining&quot;
    else:
        condition += &quot; and dry&quot;
    if wind_speed &gt;= 0 and wind_speed &lt;= 5:
        wind_type += &quot;Calm&quot; 
    elif wind_speed &gt;= 6 and wind_speed &lt;= 49:
        wind_type += &quot;Breezy&quot;
    else:
        wind_type += &quot;Windy&quot;

elif temperature &gt;= 15:
    condition = &quot;Warm&quot;
    if raining == &quot;yes&quot;:
        condition += &quot; and raining&quot;
    else:
        condition += &quot; and dry&quot;
    if wind_speed &gt;= 0 and wind_speed &lt;= 5:
        wind_type += &quot;Calm&quot; 
    elif wind_speed &gt;= 6 and wind_speed &lt;= 49:
        wind_type += &quot;Breezy&quot;
    else:
        wind_type += &quot;Windy&quot;

else:
    condition = &quot;Cold&quot;
    if raining == &quot;yes&quot;:
        condition += &quot; and raining&quot;
    else:
        condition += &quot; and dry&quot;
    if wind_speed &gt;= 0 and wind_speed &lt;= 5:
        wind_type += &quot;Calm&quot; 
    elif wind_speed &gt;= 6 and wind_speed &lt;= 49:
        wind_type += &quot;Breezy&quot;
    else:
        wind_type += &quot;Windy&quot;

print(f&quot;The weather is {condition} and {wind_type}&quot;)
</code></pre>
<p>Proposed code:</p>
<pre><code>msgs = [
    &quot;Please enter the temperature in Celsius: &quot;,
    &quot;is it raining? yes/no &quot;,
    &quot;Please enter the wind speed in km/h: &quot;
]


def get_win_state( wind_speed ):
    if wind_speed &lt;= 5:
        return &quot;Calm&quot; 
    elif wind_speed &lt;= 49:
        return &quot;Breezy&quot;
    else:
        return &quot;Windy&quot;

def get_temperature_state( temperature ):
    if temperature &gt;= 25:
       return &quot;Hot&quot;
    if temperature &gt;= 15:
        return &quot;Warm&quot;
    return &quot;Cold&quot;

def get_rain_state( state ):
    if state == &quot;yes&quot;:
        return &quot; and raining&quot;
    return &quot; and dry&quot;


print( f&quot;The weather is {get_temperature_state( float( input( msgs[ 0 ] ) ) )}{get_rain_state( input( msgs[ 1 ] ))} and {get_win_state( int( input( msgs[ 2 ] ))) }&quot; )
</code></pre>
<p>By separating the code into functions, it is easy to understand, we can reuse it, and future modifications are simpler.</p>
<p>EDIT:</p>
<p>We can simplify the previous code by creating a generic method that can be reused:</p>
<pre><code>msgs = [
    &quot;Please enter the temperature in Celsius: &quot;,
    &quot;is it raining? yes/no &quot;,
    &quot;Please enter the wind speed in km/h: &quot;
]

limits = [
    [ 25, 15 ],
    [ 49, 5 ]
]

values = [
    [ &quot;Hot&quot;, &quot;Warm&quot;, &quot;Cold&quot; ],
    [ &quot;Windy&quot;, &quot;Breezy&quot;, &quot;Calm&quot; ]
]

def get_state( user_input, file ):
    for i in range( len( limits[ file ] )):
        if user_input &gt; limits[ file ][ i ]:
            return values[ file ][ i ]

    return values[ file ][ -1 ]

def get_rain_state( state ):
    if state == &quot;yes&quot;:
        return &quot; and raining&quot;
    return &quot; and dry&quot;


print( f&quot;The weather is {get_state( float( input( msgs[ 0 ] ) ), 0 )}{get_rain_state( input( msgs[ 1 ] ))} and {get_state( int( input( msgs[ 2 ] )), 1 ) }&quot; )
</code></pre>
<p>Last edit:</p>
<pre><code>msgs = [
    &quot;Please enter the temperature in Celsius: &quot;,

      # we modified the message to the user so that we could
      # use the &quot;get_state()&quot; method in all entries.
    &quot;Plase enter a number &gt; 1 is it raining &quot;,
    &quot;Please enter the wind speed in km/h: &quot;
]

limits = [
    [ 25, 15 ],
    [ 1, 0 ],
    [ 49, 5 ]
]

values = [
    [ &quot;Hot&quot;, &quot;Warm&quot;, &quot;Cold&quot; ],
    [ &quot; and raining and &quot;, &quot; and dry and &quot; ],
    [ &quot;Windy&quot;, &quot;Breezy&quot;, &quot;Calm&quot; ]
]

  # determines what data we will use in each entry
file = 0

def get_state( user_input ):
    global file
    for i in range( len( limits[ file ] )):
        if user_input &gt; limits[ file ][ i ]:
            return values[ file ][ i ]

    return values[ file ][ -1 ]

  # we establish what the strings entered by the user will be converted to
paremeters_types = [
    float,
    int,
    int
]

def get_user_input():
    return paremeters_types[ file ]( input( msgs[ file ] ) )

def show_wheater():
    global file
    weather_report = &quot;The weather is &quot;
    while file &lt; len( paremeters_types ):
        weather_report += get_state( get_user_input() )
        file += 1
    return weather_report

print( show_wheater() )
</code></pre>
<p>As you can see, it would be very simple to add, for example, a <strong>humidity</strong> parameter. You would only have to add the corresponding lists in <strong>msgs</strong>, <strong>limits</strong>, <strong>values</strong> y <strong>paremeters_types</strong>, the rest is done by the code itself.</p>
